\begin_layout Title
A Short Review Of The 1972 Freedman-Clauser And 1982 Aspect Experiments:
 An Empirical Mapping of Continuous Attenuation Functions vs.
 Discrete Binary Products.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
This article reviews the results of the 1972 Freedman-Clauser 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Freedman1972"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 experiments and the 1982 Aspect et.
 al.
 experiments 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Aspect82"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
The results of both experiments are in excellent agreement with their relevant expectation values.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Stuart Freedman's thesis 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Freedman1972"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 thesis and Alain Aspect's article
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Aspect82"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 both describe the measurement of the polarization correlation of two photons emitted in the 
\begin_inset Formula $4p^{2}\;^{1}S_{0}\rightarrow4s4p^{1}P_{1}\rightarrow4s^{2}\;^{1}S_{0}$
\end_inset

 cascade of calcium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The results of both experiments are in excellent agreement with their relevant expectation values.
 Unfortunately this situation is somewhat marred by comparisons with inapplicable expectation values.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The Two-Photon System Investigated
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The systems investigated by Freedman's and Aspect's experiments both comprise beams of distinguishable pairs of photons emitted in an atomic cascade.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To a good approximation,
 the atom cascades via electric dipole transitions through angular-momentum states 
\begin_inset Formula $J=0\rightarrow J=1\rightarrow J=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The initial and final atomic angular momentum.
 is zero;
 hence,
 by conservation of angular momentum,
 the two photons are in a combined state of angular momentum zero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Relevant Theory
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Freedman incorrectly states that the expectation value for the product of two individual spin measurements 
\begin_inset Formula $A\left(\hat{a},\lambda_{1}\right)B\left(\hat{b},\lambda_{2}\right)=\pm$
\end_inset

1 in the directions 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{a}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{b}$
\end_inset

 is given by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after "equation (I.2)"
key "Freedman1972"
literal "false"

\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
E\left(\hat{a},\hat{b}\right) & = & \left\langle \psi\left|\hat{a}\cdot\overrightarrow{\sigma_{1}}\,\hat{b}\cdot\overrightarrow{\sigma_{2}}\right|\psi\right\rangle =-\hat{a}\cdot\hat{b}\label{eq:FI.2}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\psi$
\end_inset

 represents the wavefunction of two photons in the so-called singlet state.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:FI.2"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 is actually the expectation value for the observables 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{a}\cdot\overrightarrow{\sigma_{1}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{b}\cdot\overrightarrow{\sigma_{2}}$
\end_inset

 operating on the wavefunction 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi$
\end_inset

 whereas the expectaion value of 
\begin_inset Formula $A\left(\hat{a},\lambda_{1}\right)B\left(\hat{b},\lambda_{2}\right)$
\end_inset

 is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
E\left(A\left(\hat{a},\lambda_{1}\right)B\left(\hat{b},\lambda_{2}\right)\right)_{\Lambda} & = & \int_{\Lambda}\rho\left(\lambda\right)A\left(\hat{a},\lambda_{1}\right)B\left(\hat{b},\lambda_{2}\right)d\lambda_{2}d\lambda_{1}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

were:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 represents the random variable 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \lambda_{1},\lambda_{2}\right\} $
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\rho\left(\lambda\right)$
\end_inset

 is the probability distribution function of the random variables 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\Lambda$
\end_inset

 is the population space of the set of random variables 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \lambda_{1},\lambda_{2}\right\} $
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Freedman derives the following inequalities 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after "inequality (II.8)"
key "Freedman1972"
literal "false"

\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
-1\leq & \frac{R\left(\phi_{1}\right)}{R_{0}}-\frac{R\left(\phi_{1}+\phi_{2}+\phi_{3}\right)}{R_{0}}+\frac{R\left(\phi_{2}\right)}{R_{0}}+\frac{R\left(\phi_{3}\right)}{R_{0}}-\frac{R_{1}}{R_{0}}-\frac{R_{2}}{R_{0}} & \leq0\label{eq:FII.8}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\phi_{1} & = & cos^{-1}\left(\hat{a},\hat{b}\right)\\
\phi_{2} & = & cos^{-1}\left(\hat{d},\hat{c}\right)\\
\phi_{3} & = & cos^{-1}\left(\hat{d},\hat{b}\right)
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\phi_{1}+\phi_{2}+\phi_{3} & = & cos^{-1}\left(\hat{a},\hat{c}\right)
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $R\left(\phi_{i}\right)$
\end_inset

 representsthe coincidence rate with both polarizers in place.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $R_{0}$
\end_inset

 representsthe coincidence rate with both polarizers removed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $R_{1}$
\end_inset

 represents the coincidence rate with polarizer 2 removed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $R_{2}$
\end_inset

 represents the coincidence rate with polarizer 1 removed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Freedman derives the expectation value for the experimental results as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
E\left(\frac{R\left(\phi\right)}{R_{0}}\right) & = & \frac{1}{4}\left(\varepsilon^{1}_{M}+\varepsilon^{1}_{m}\right)\left(\varepsilon^{2}_{M}+\varepsilon^{2}_{m}\right)+\frac{1}{4}\left(\varepsilon^{1}_{M}-\varepsilon^{1}_{m}\right)\left(\varepsilon^{2}_{M}-\varepsilon^{2}_{m}\right)cos\left(2\phi\right)\label{eq:FII.10}\\
E\left(\frac{R_{1}}{R_{0}}\right) & = & \frac{1}{2}\left(\varepsilon^{1}_{M}+\varepsilon^{1}_{m}\right)\label{eq:FII.11a}\\
E\left(\frac{R_{2}}{R_{0}}\right) & = & \frac{1}{2}\left(\varepsilon^{2}_{M}+\varepsilon^{2}_{m}\right)\label{eq:FI.11b}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\phi$
\end_inset

 represents the angle between the two polarizer orientations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon^{i}_{M}$
\end_inset

 represents the transmission efficiency of polarizer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon^{i}_{m}$
\end_inset

 represents the non-transmission efficiency of polarizer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Experimental Descriptions 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The experiments of Freedman 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after "Section F. Outline of the Experiment"
key "Freedman1972"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and Aspect are similar comprizing polarizers,
 detectors and coincidence monitoring devices.
 The main difference being in the type of polarizers used.
 Freedman used pile-of-plates polarizers for which only main ray photons could be detected whereas Aspect used dichotomic polarizers enabling detection of both main and extrordinary ray photons.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In both experiments photons are emitted in an atomic cascade and are counted in coincidence,
 the coincidence rate being observed as a function of the individual photon polarization states.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In Freedman's experiment the following quantities were measured measured:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $R\left(\phi\right)$
\end_inset

:
 the coincidence rate for two-photon detection as a function of the angle 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi$
\end_inset

 between the planes of linear polarization defined by the orientation of the two inserted polarizers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $R_{1}$
\end_inset

:
 the coincidence rate with polarizer 2 removed;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $R_{2}$
\end_inset

:
 the coincidence rate with polarizer 1 removed;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $R_{0}$
\end_inset

:
 the coincidence rate with both polarizers removed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These rates are determined by correcting the observed coincidence rates for the random coincidence background.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In addition to ordinary coincidence counting,
 a time delay-coincidence system provides a spectrum of the arrival time difference between 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{2}$
\end_inset

 which gives the lifetime of the 
\begin_inset Formula $4s4p^{1}P_{1}$
\end_inset

 state and provides a check on the consistency of the experiment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In Aspects experiment the following quantities were measured:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $R_{\pm\pm}\left(\overrightarrow{a},\overrightarrow{b}\right)$
\end_inset

:
 four coincidence counting rates for 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ +,+\right\} $
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ +,-\right\} $
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ -,+\right\} $
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ -,-\right\} $
\end_inset

 pair detections.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Experimental Results
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The primary experimental metric was defined as the normalized coincidence ratio:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{R(\phi)}{R_{0}} & = & \frac{1}{4}\left\{ (\epsilon^{1}_{1}+\epsilon^{2}_{1})(\epsilon^{1}_{2}+\epsilon^{2}_{2})+(\epsilon^{1}_{1}-\epsilon^{2}_{1})(\epsilon^{1}_{2}-\epsilon^{2}_{2})F(0)\cos(2\phi)\right\} 
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Where:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
• 
\begin_inset Formula $R(\phi)$
\end_inset

 is the integrated coincidence count rate at relative polarizer alignment 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi=\vert a-b\vert$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
• 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{0}$
\end_inset

 is the baseline reference rate measured with both polarizing plates physically removed from the optical paths.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
• 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon_{1},\epsilon_{2}$
\end_inset

 represent continuous laboratory calibration constants tracking maximum transmittance and minimum leakage.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Conclusion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Neither Freedman's nor Aspect's experiment satisfies the fundamental probability constraints mandated by Bell’s 1964 Equation (2).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In both experiments the empirical data points track a smooth,
 solid continuous curve 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{\langle R(\phi)/R_{0}\rangle}$
\end_inset

 because the laboratory instruments are measuring continuous macroscopic wave intensities and timing intervals.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Continuous profile divergence from standard CHSH inequalities mathematically demonstrates that the inequality is completely inapplicable to this class of data;
 not that nature has violated local realistic probability logic.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Unfortunately the statistical concept and definition of expectation value appeares to be widely misunderstood or ignored by many within the physics community.
\end_layout

